       | ### 3.  Divisors of \(1+x^{d}\)
       | 
       | Factorisation of \(1+x^{d}\) over \(\mathbb Z\) is well‑known:
       | 
       | \[
       | 1+x^{d}= \prod_{\substack{e\mid 2d\\ e\nmid d}}\Phi_{e}(x),
       | \]
       | 
       | where \(\Phi_{e}\) is the \(e\)-th cyclotomic polynomial.
       | All factors are distinct and each \(\Phi_{e}\) is monic with constant term \(1\).
       | 
       | Consequently every divisor of a polynomial \(1+x^{d}\) is a product of a
       | subset of those cyclotomic factors.
       | All cyclotomic polynomials that can appear have degree \(\varphi(e)\le 8\).
       | Listing all even \(e\) with \(\varphi(e)\le 8\) gives  
       | 
       | \[
       | \begin{array}{c|c}
       | e & \Phi_{e}(x) \\ \hline
       |  2 & x+1 \\ 
       |  4 & x^{2}+1 \\ 
       |  6 & x^{2}-x+1 \\ 
       |  8 & x^{4}+1 \\ 
       | 10 & x^{4}-x^{3}+x^{2}-x+1 \\ 
       | 12 & x^{4}-x^{2}+1 \\ 
       | 14 & x^{6}-x^{5}+x^{4}-x^{3}+x^{2}-x+1 \\ 
       | 16 & x^{8}+1 \\ 
       | 18 & x^{6}-x^{3}+1 \\ 
       | 20 & x^{8}-x^{6}+x^{4}-x^{2}+1 \\ 
       | 24 & x^{8}-x^{4}+1 \\ 
       | 30 & x^{8}+x^{7}-x^{5}-x^{4}-x^{3}+x+1
       | \end{array}
       | \]
       | 
       | The exponent of the factor \(2\) in each index \(e\) is the same for all
       | factors that can occur together in a divisor of a single \(1+x^{d}\).
       | Thus we group the above by the 2‑adic valuation \(v_{2}(e)\):
       | 
       | * \(v_{2}=1\):  \(e=2,6,10,14,18,30\) (degrees \(1,2,4,6,6,8\));
       | * \(v_{2}=2\):  \(e=4,12,20\) (degrees \(2,4,8\));
       | * \(v_{2}=3\):  \(e=8,24\) (degrees \(4,8\));
       | * \(v_{2}=4\):  \(e=16\) (degree \(8\)).
       | 
       | A divisor \(B\) is obtained by choosing any subset of the cyclotomic
       | polynomials inside a single group; the degree of \(B\) is the sum of the
       | chosen \(\varphi(e)\).  
       | Counting all subsets whose total degree does not exceed \(8\) gives
       | 
       | \[
       | \begin{array}{c|c}
       | v_{2} & \#\text{ of admissible }B \\ \hline
       | 1 & 15\\
       | 2 & 5\\
       | 3 & 3\\
       | 4 & 2
       | \end{array}
       | \]
       | 
       | (the empty subset corresponds to the constant polynomial \(1\)).
       | The constant polynomial has been counted once, so the total number of
       | different \(B\) is  
