       | assistantfinalThe numbers on the board will always be non‑negative integers.  
       | Let  
       | 
       | * \(S\) = sum of all numbers on the board,  
       | * \(M\) = the largest number on the board,  
       | * \(n\) = the amount of numbers on the board.
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 1.  When does the game stop ?
       | 
       | Condition (1) is  
       | 
       | \[
       | \text{“some number is larger than the sum of the others’’}
       | \Longleftrightarrow M > S-M \Longleftrightarrow 2M > S .
       | \]
       | 
       | Thus the game stops as soon as \(2M>S\).
       | 
       | Condition (2) (all zeros) can only occur after the sum has become
       | \(S=0\); then \(M=0\) and the game also stops.  
       | Because the sum never increases (see below), condition (2) can never be
       | reached earlier than condition (1) unless the sum is already \(0\).
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 2.  Behaviour of the sum
       | 
       | If the coin shows **heads**, the number written is \(x+y\); the total sum
       | does not change:
       | 
       | \[
       | S' = S-x-y+(x+y)=S .
       | \]
       | 
       | If the coin shows **tails**, the number written is \(|x-y|\); the sum
       | decreases by \(2\min (x,y)\):
       | 
       | \[
       | S' = S-x-y+|x-y| = S-2\min (x,y)\le S .
       | \]
       | 
       | Hence the sum never increases; it stays \(1997\) if every round is heads,
       | and it becomes smaller whenever a tail occurs.
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 3.  The hardest case for player \(A\)
       | 
       | Player \(A\) wants the game to finish **as early as possible** (more
       | numbers left ⇒ more cookies).  
       | Since a tail only *decreases* the sum, it can only make the inequality
       | \(2M>S\) easier to satisfy.  
       | Consequently the worst situation for \(A\) is when **all coin flips are
       | heads**; then \(S\) stays equal to the initial sum \(1997\).
       | 
       | With \(S=1997\) condition (1) requires  
       | 
       | \[
       | M\ge \Bigl\lfloor\frac{1997}{2}\Bigr\rfloor+1 = 999 .
       | \]
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 4.  How many rounds are needed to obtain a number \(\ge 999\)?
       | 
       | Every round removes two numbers and puts back one, so the number of
       | numbers on the board decreases by exactly one each round.
       | To obtain a number of size at least \(999\) we must combine at least
       | \(999\) copies of the initial \(1\)’s into a single number.
       | Merging \(k\) copies into one needs \(k-1\) rounds; therefore at least  
       | 
       | \[
       | 999-1 = 998
       | \]
       | 
       | rounds are necessary, no matter how the pairs are chosen.
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 5.  Player \(A\) can achieve this bound
       | 
       | Assume the coin shows heads (the worst case).  
       | At each round \(A\) erases the current largest number \(M\) and a
       | \(1\). The new number is \(M+1\).  
       | After \(t\) such rounds we have the numbers  
       | 
       | \[
       | M=t+1,\qquad\text{and }1997-(t+1)\text{ copies of }1 .
       | \]
       | 
       | After \(t=998\) rounds the largest number is \(999\); the total sum is
       | still \(1997\) and  
       | 
       | \[
       | 2\cdot 999 = 1998 > 1997 .
       | \]
       | 
       | Thus condition (1) is satisfied exactly after the \(998^{\text{th}}\)
       | round.  
       | 
       | If a tail ever occurs, \(A\) simply erases two equal \(1\)’s, producing a
       | \(0\); this reduces the sum while leaving the largest number unchanged,
       | so the game can only finish **earlier**, never later.  
       | 
       | Hence the longest the game can be forced to last (the optimal play of
       | \(B\)) is \(998\) rounds.
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 6.  Number of cookies
       | 
       | The game starts with \(1997\) numbers and loses one number each round.
       | After \(998\) rounds the number of remaining numbers is  
       | 
       | \[
       | 1997-998 = 999 .
       | \]
       | 
       | Therefore player \(A\) receives **999 cookies**.
       | 
