       | 
       | In each round  
       | 
       | * player \(A\) erases two numbers \(x,y\);  
       | * a coin decides the operation that player \(B\) must apply  
       | 
       | \[
       | \text{heads}\;:\;x+y ,\qquad 
       | \text{tails}\;:\;|x-y|.
       | \]
       | 
       | After the operation the two erased numbers are replaced by the single number
       | produced, so the number of entries on the board is reduced by one each
       | round.
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 1.  The game cannot end by condition (2)
       | 
       | Let \(S\) be the sum of the numbers on the board.
       | If the coin shows heads, the sum is unchanged; if it shows tails the sum is
       | reduced by \(2\min (x,y)\), an even number.
       | Hence the parity of the sum never changes.
       | Initially \(S=1997\) (odd), therefore \(S\) can never become \(0\).  
       | Consequently the game can end only by condition (1).
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 2.  A useful inequality
       | 
       | Denote after \(t\) rounds  
       | 
       | * \(n\) – the number of entries left \((n=N-t)\);
       | * \(S\) – the current sum;
       | * \(M\) – the largest entry.
       | 
       | If condition (1) holds then  
       | 
       | \[
       | M > S-M\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad M>\frac S2 .
       | \tag{1}
       | \]
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 3.  Upper bound for the largest entry
       | 
       | During a **sum** round the new entry is \(x+y\); the largest entry can
       | increase by at most the smallest erased number.
       | Player \(A\) can always choose the smallest number on the board, which is
       | at least \(1\).  
       | Thus each sum round can increase the current maximum by **at most \(1\)**.
       | 
       | During a **difference** round the new entry is \(|x-y|\le\max\{x,y\}\); the
       | maximum never increases.
       | 
       | Consequently after \(s\) sum‑rounds (and any number of difference rounds)
       | 
       | \[
       | M\le 1+s .\tag{2}
       | \]
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 4.  Lower bound for the total sum
       | 
       | A difference round reduces the sum by \(2\min (x,y)\).  
       | By always choosing the smallest number (which is at least \(1\)) the
       | reduction is at most \(2\).  Hence after \(d\) difference rounds
       | 
       | \[
       | S\ge N-2d .\tag{3}
       | \]
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 5.  How many rounds are necessary for condition (1)?
       | 
       | Let the total number of rounds be \(t=s+d\).
       | Using (2) and (3) in (1) we obtain the necessary condition for the game
       | to be able to finish:
       | 
       | \[
       | 1+s \;>\; \frac{N-2d}{2}
       | \Longrightarrow
       | 2+2s \;>\; N-2d .
       | \]
       | 
       | Since \(s=t-d\),
       | 
       | \[
       | 2+2(t-d) \;>\; N-2d
       | \Longrightarrow
       | 2+2t \;>\; N .
       | \]
       | 
       | Thus
       | 
       | \[
       | t>\frac{N-2}{2}.
       | \tag{4}
       | \]
       | 
       | Inequality (4) shows that **at least \(\left\lfloor\frac N2\right\rfloor\) rounds
       | must be played** before condition (1) can ever be satisfied,
       | no matter how player \(B\) chooses the coin outcomes.
       | Because each round removes one entry, the number of entries that can
       | remain at the end is at least
       | 
       | \[
       | N-\Bigl\lfloor\frac N2\Bigr\rfloor
       |    =\Bigl\lceil\frac N2\Bigr\rceil .
       | \tag{5}
       | \]
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 6.  A strategy for player \(A\) that attains this bound
       | 
       | Player \(A\) can force the game to end exactly after
       | \(\left\lfloor\frac N2\right\rfloor\) rounds, i.e. with
       | \(\lceil N/2\rceil\) numbers left.
       | 
       | **Strategy.**  
       | Whenever the coin shows *heads* (so a sum must be written), \(A\) erases a
       | \(1\) (the smallest number) and the current largest number.
       | When the coin shows *tails* (a difference must be written), \(A\) erases a
       | \(1\) and any other number; the result is again a non‑negative integer not
       | larger than the largest one.
       | 
       | With this strategy the board always contains
       | 
       | * one “large’’ number \(L\) and
       | * the remaining entries are all \(1\)’s (or \(0\)’s after a difference on
       | equal numbers).
       | 
       | After \(t\) rounds the large number equals \(L=1+t\) and the number of
       | \(1\)’s left is \(N-t-1\).  
       | The condition \(L> \text{sum of the others}\) becomes  
       | 
       | \[
       | 1+t > N-t-1 \Longleftrightarrow t>\frac{N-2}{2},
       | \]
       | 
       | which is exactly the bound (4).  
       | Hence the game stops after the smallest integer \(t\) satisfying (4),
       | namely \(t=\bigl\lfloor N/2\bigr\rfloor\).  
       | The remaining number of entries is \(N-t = \lceil N/2\rceil\).
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 7.  Optimal result
       | 
       | Both players are optimal:
       | * Player \(B\) cannot force the game to end earlier than (5);
       | * Player \(A\) can guarantee that the game ends exactly at (5).
       | 
       | Therefore the number of cookies that player \(A\) receives is
       | 
       | \[
       | \boxed{\left\lceil\frac{1997}{2}\right\rceil}= \boxed{999}.
       | \]

