       | ---
       | 
       | ### 1.  Form of a geometric polynomial  
       | 
       | If a polynomial \(P(x)\) has an integer root \(a\) of multiplicity \(n\ge 2\), then  
       | 
       | \[
       | P(x)=A\,(B x+C)^{\,n},
       | \]
       | 
       | with integers \(A,B,C\) (the factor \(B x+C\) is a linear change of variable).  
       | 
       | Indeed, taking integers \(t,r\) with \(|r|>1\) and letting  
       | 
       | \[
       | x_k=\frac{t r^{k}-C}{B}\qquad(k=0,1,2,\dots)
       | \]
       | 
       | gives  
       | 
       | \[
       | P(x_k)=A\,(t r^{k})^{n}=A t^{\,n}\,(r^{n})^{k},
       | \]
       | 
       | which is a geometric progression.  
       | Conversely, if a polynomial produces an infinite geometric progression,
       | its values must be of the above form; otherwise a non‑constant factor would
       | have infinitely many equal values, impossible for a polynomial.
       | 
       | Thus a geometric polynomial is exactly
       | 
       | \[
       | P(x)=A\,(B x+C)^{n},\qquad A,B,C\in\mathbb Z,\;n\ge 2 .
       | \]
       | 
       | ---
       | 
       | ### 2.  Use the given leading coefficient and constant term  
       | 
