       | \[
       | 0\le a_i\le 1 ,\qquad \sum_{i=1}^{d}a_i = 59 .
       | \]
       | 
       | A group (bin) may contain any collection of students whose total score does not exceed \(1\).
       | Denote by  
       | 
       | * \(L\) – the set of students with score \(> \tfrac12\) (large items),  
       | * \(S\) – the set of students with score \(\le \tfrac12\) (small items).
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 1.  Upper bound for the number of groups
       | 
       | Each student in \(L\) must be placed in a different group, because two scores
       | \(> \tfrac12\) would already exceed the capacity \(1\).
       | Hence at least \(|L|\) groups are needed.
       | 
       | Let  
       | 
       | \[
       | \ell = |L| ,\qquad
       | \Sigma_L = \sum_{i\in L} a_i ,\qquad
       | \Sigma_S = 59-\Sigma_L .
       | \]
       | 
       | The remaining capacity in the \(\ell\) groups that already contain a large
       | student equals  
       | 
       | \[
       | \ell - \Sigma_L .
       | \]
       | 
       | If \(\Sigma_S\le \ell-\Sigma_L\) we can put all small scores into those groups,
       | so the total number of groups needed is \(\ell\).
       | 
       | Otherwise we need extra groups for the excess small score
       | 
       | \[
       | \Sigma_S-(\ell-\Sigma_L)=59-\ell .
       | \]
       | 
       | Thus the total number of groups required is at most  
       | 
       | \[
       | \ell + \max\{0,\,59-\ell\}= \max\{\ell,59\}.
       | \]
       | 
       | Because each large score exceeds \(\tfrac12\),
       | 
       | \[
       | \Sigma_L > \tfrac12\ell\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
       | \tfrac12\ell < 59\;\Longrightarrow\; \ell <118 .
       | \]
       | 
       | Hence \(\ell\le 117\).  
       | Consequently the number of groups never exceeds  
       | 
       | \[
       | \max\{117,59\}=117 .
       | \]
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 2.  A configuration that forces 117 groups
       | 
       | Take a small number \(\varepsilon>0\) with \(\varepsilon<\dfrac{1}{236}\) and set  
       | 
       | \[
       | \begin{aligned}
       | &\text{for }i=1,\dots ,117:& a_i &= \tfrac12+\varepsilon,\\[2mm]
       | &\text{one more student:}& a_{118}&= \tfrac12-117\varepsilon .
       | \end{aligned}
       | \]
       | 
       | All scores lie in \([0,1]\) and  
       | 
       | \[
       | 117\Bigl(\tfrac12+\varepsilon\Bigr)+\Bigl(\tfrac12-117\varepsilon\Bigr)=59 .
       | \]
       | 
       | Each of the 117 scores \(>\tfrac12\) must occupy a distinct group.
       | The remaining student can be placed together with any one of them, but this does
       | not reduce the number of groups below 117.  
       | Thus this instance **requires** 117 groups.
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 3.  Minimal guaranteed number of groups
       | 
       | The previous construction shows that 117 groups are sometimes necessary,
       | while the argument in part 1 shows that 117 groups always suffice.
       | 
       | \[
       | \boxed{117}
       | \]

