       | 
       | \[
       | a_1,a_2,\dots ,a_d\in[0,1],\qquad \sum_{i=1}^{d}a_i =59 .
       | \]
       | 
       | We want a partition of the students into \(k\) groups (bins) whose total score does not exceed \(1\) in any group.  
       | The question asks for the smallest integer \(k\) that guarantees such a partition for **every** possible multiset
       | \(\{a_i\}\).
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | 
       | ### 1.  How many bins can be forced to be used?
       | 
       | Call a student **large** if his score is \(> \tfrac12\).  
       | Two large students cannot be placed in the same group, because their sum would exceed \(1\).
       | 
       | Let  
       | 
       | * \(n\) = number of large students,
       | * \(R = 59-\sum_{\text{large}} a_i\) = total score of the remaining (small) students
       |   (each of them has score \(\le \tfrac12\)).
       | 
       | All large students must occupy different groups, so we already need \(n\) groups.
       | The small students together have total score \(R\); they can be packed into at most
       | \(\lceil R\rceil\) groups (just fill each group up to capacity \(1\)).
       | Hence the total number of groups needed satisfies  
       | 
       | \[
       | \boxed{B\le n+\lceil R\rceil } .
       | \tag{1}
       | \]
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | 
       | ### 2.  Bounding \(B\) from above
       | 
       | Since every large student has score \(> \tfrac12\),
       | 
       | \[
       | \sum_{\text{large}} a_i > \tfrac12 n\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
       | R = 59-\sum_{\text{large}} a_i < 59-\tfrac12 n .
       | \]
       | 
       | Insert this inequality into (1):
       | 
       | \[
       | B < n+\Big\lceil 59-\tfrac12 n\Big\rceil .
       | \]
       | 
       | For a real number \(x\),
       | 
       | \[
       | \lceil x-\varepsilon\rceil=
       | \begin{cases}
       | \lfloor x\rfloor, & x\notin\mathbb Z,\\[2pt]
       | x-1, & x\in\mathbb Z,
       | \end{cases}
       | \qquad (\varepsilon>0\text{ arbitrarily small}).
       | \]
       | 
       | Thus
       | 
       | \[
       | B\le 
       | \begin{cases}
       | n+ \bigl(59-\tfrac12 n\bigr)=59+\tfrac12 n, & 59-\tfrac12 n\notin\mathbb Z,\\[4pt]
       | n+ \bigl(59-\tfrac12 n-1\bigr)=58+\tfrac12 n, & 59-\tfrac12 n\in\mathbb Z .
       | \end{cases}
       | \]
       | 
       | Since \(\tfrac12 n\) is either an integer (when \(n\) is even) or ends with \(.5\) (when \(n\) is odd),
       | the two cases combine to the simple formula  
       | 
       | \[
       | \boxed{B\le 59+\Big\lfloor \frac{n}{2}\Big\rfloor } .
       | \tag{2}
       | \]
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | 
       | ### 3.  The largest possible \(n\)
       | 
       | Because each large student contributes more than \(\tfrac12\) to the total score,
       | the number of large students satisfies  
       | 
       | \[
       | \frac12 n < 59 \;\Longrightarrow\; n\le 117 .
       | \]
       | 
       | (The value \(n=118\) would already give a total exceeding \(59\).)
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | 
       | ### 4.  The worst‑case number of groups
       | 
       | Insert the maximal possible \(n=117\) into (2):
       | 
       | \[
       | B_{\max}\le 59+\Big\lfloor\frac{117}{2}\Big\rfloor
       |           = 59+58 = 117 .
       | \]
       | 
       | Hence **no matter how the scores are distributed, 117 groups always suffice**.
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | 
       | ### 5.  Sharpness – an instance that needs 117 groups
       | 
       | Take \(117\) students, each with score  
       | 
       | \[
       | a_i=\frac{59}{117}\approx 0.5042735\ (> \tfrac12).
       | \]
       | 
       | Their total score is exactly \(59\).  
       | Because each score exceeds \(\tfrac12\), no two of them can be placed together,
       | so every student must occupy a separate group.  
       | Thus any feasible partition needs at least \(117\) groups.
       | 
       | Therefore the bound \(117\) is attainable and cannot be lowered.
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | 
       | \[
       | \boxed{\,k=117\,}
       | \]
       | 
       | So the smallest integer \(k\) that guarantees a partition of any class of total score \(59\) into groups whose sums do not exceed \(1\) is **117**.

