       | be the two‑digit representation of a positive integer \(n\) in base \(b\).
       | If \(n\) is \(b\)-eautiful then  
       | 
       | \[
       | a+c=\sqrt n .
       | \]
       | 
       | Write \(\sqrt n =k\;(k\in\mathbb Z_{>0})\).  
       | Then  
       | 
       | \[
       | k^{2}=ab+c,\qquad a+c=k .
       | \]
       | 
       | From the second equation \(c=k-a\); substituting in the first gives  
       | 
       | \[
       | k^{2}=ab+k-a\Longrightarrow k^{2}-k=a(b-1).
       | \]
       | 
       | Hence  
       | 
       | \[
       | a=\frac{k(k-1)}{b-1}. \tag{1}
       | \]
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 1.  When does a solution exist?
       | 
       | Because \(\gcd(k,k-1)=1\), the integer \((b-1)\) must divide the product
       | \(k(k-1)\).  
       | Write  
       | 
       | \[
       | b-1=uv,\qquad \gcd(u,v)=1 .
       | \]
       | 
       | All prime powers of \(b-1\) must be placed wholly in either \(u\) or \(v\).
       | Thus each factorisation \(b-1=uv\) with \(\gcd(u,v)=1\) corresponds to a
       | solution of the simultaneous congruences  
       | 
       | \[
       | k\equiv 0\pmod u,\qquad k\equiv 1\pmod v .
       | \]
       | 
       | By the Chinese Remainder Theorem there is a unique \(k\;(0<k<b-1)\)
       | satisfying them, and (1) gives an integer \(a\).  
       | Conversely, any \(k\) that makes (1) integral yields such a coprime
       | decomposition of \(b-1\).
       | 
       | Thus the number of admissible pairs \((a,c)\) (hence the number of
       | \(b\)-eautiful integers) equals the number of ordered coprime factor
       | pairs \((u,v)\) of \(b-1\) **minus one**, because the pair \((u,v)=(1,b-1)\)
       | gives \(k=1\) and then \(a=0\), which is not allowed (the leading digit
       | must be non‑zero).
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 2.  Counting the factor pairs
       | 
       | If  
       | 
       | \[
       | b-1 = p_{1}^{e_{1}}p_{2}^{e_{2}}\dots p_{r}^{e_{r}}
       | \]
       | 
       | has \(r\) distinct prime factors, each prime power must be assigned
       | either to \(u\) or to \(v\).  Hence the number of ordered coprime pairs
