       | \[
       | (x,y)=\bigl(p(1-s),\,qs\bigr),\qquad 0\le s\le 1 .
       | \]
       | 
       | Hence for a given \((x,y)\) there is a parameter \(s\) with  
       | 
       | \[
       | p=\frac{x}{1-s},\qquad q=\frac{y}{s},
       | \]
       | 
       | and the condition \(p^{2}+q^{2}=1\) becomes  
       | 
       | \[
       | \frac{x^{2}}{(1-s)^{2}}+\frac{y^{2}}{s^{2}}=1\qquad (0<s<1). \tag{1}
       | \]
       | 
       | For fixed \((x,y)\) the left side of (1) is a convex function of \(s\) that
       | tends to \(+\infty\) as \(s\to0^{+}\) or \(s\to1^{-}\); therefore it has a
       | unique minimum at the point where its derivative vanishes:
       | 
       | \[
       | \frac{d}{ds}\Bigl(\frac{x^{2}}{(1-s)^{2}}+\frac{y^{2}}{s^{2}}\Bigr)=
       | \frac{2x^{2}}{(1-s)^{3}}-\frac{2y^{2}}{s^{3}}=0 .
       | \]
       | 
       | Thus the minimizing value of \(s\) is  
       | 
       | \[
       | s^{*}= \frac{y^{2/3}}{x^{2/3}+y^{2/3}} . \tag{2}
       | \]
       | 
       | If for a point \((x,y)\) the equation (1) has **more than one** solution
       | \(s\), then the minimum value of the left side is \(<1\); consequently there
