       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 1.  Rectangles in a regular dodecagon  
       | 
       | If four vertices form a rectangle, each interior angle is \(90^{\circ}\).  
       | By Thales’ theorem a right angle subtended by a chord must have that chord
       | as a diameter; hence the two diagonals of an inscribed rectangle are
       | diameters of the circle.  
       | 
       | Thus the four vertices of any rectangle are exactly the endpoints of two
       | distinct diameters \(D_i\) and \(D_j\;(i\neq j)\).
       | 
       | Consequently a monochromatic rectangle occurs **iff** there are two
       | different diameters whose four endpoints are all the same colour.
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 2.  Colouring the diameters  
       | 
       | Each diameter \(D_i\) contains two vertices, so for a given colour
       | assignment the pair \((i,i+6)\) can be
       | 
       | * \(RR\) – both red,
       | * \(BB\) – both blue,
       | * \(RB\) – red then blue,
       | * \(BR\) – blue then red.
       | 
       | These four possibilities are independent for the six diameters, giving
       | \(4^{6}=2^{12}=4096\) colourings in total.
       | 
